1
00:00:00,640 --> 00:00:04,770
From the 12th of November 2026, there
is a new rule about how you are allowed

2
00:00:04,810 --> 00:00:09,060
to talk to Claude. Anthropic updated
its usage policy and now you cannot be

3
00:00:09,080 --> 00:00:11,170
abusive or cruel to Claude.

4
00:00:11,640 --> 00:00:15,230
And just to be clear, I'm not here trying
to fight for the right to be cruel

5
00:00:15,290 --> 00:00:18,540
or abusive to AI. So this is
not a "do I want to abuse

6
00:00:18,910 --> 00:00:22,800
it, do you want to abuse it, should we
abuse or not abuse it or be cruel to

7
00:00:22,820 --> 00:00:26,730
it" kind of matter. What I want to
talk about is like the actual thought

8
00:00:27,750 --> 00:00:32,070
process behind this rule. Part of the idea
is that Anthropic is not sure whether

9
00:00:32,090 --> 00:00:35,070
Claude might be conscious. So
they think, they are not sure,

10
00:00:35,090 --> 00:00:36,930
but they think that Claude
might be conscious.

11
00:00:37,440 --> 00:00:40,280
So what does Anthropic's
new rule actually say?

12
00:00:40,340 --> 00:00:44,857
The exact wording is actually: bans
sustained and needless abusive or cruel

13
00:00:44,877 --> 00:00:46,640
behaviour towards their models.

14
00:00:46,820 --> 00:00:50,400
And of course, according to them, this
is only reserved for extreme cases.

15
00:00:50,420 --> 00:00:54,140
And the main way they enforce it is
that Claude can end the conversation

16
00:00:54,580 --> 00:00:58,640
if it starts to seem as though the other
person on the other side is abusive

17
00:00:58,660 --> 00:01:02,090
or cruel to it. Then in
Claude's constitution,

18
00:01:02,500 --> 00:01:06,280
the document that Anthropic published
to describe who Claude should be.

19
00:01:06,300 --> 00:01:09,340
They call Claude's moral
status deeply uncertain.

20
00:01:09,700 --> 00:01:12,393
So they're not explicitly saying
that, oh, Claude is conscious,

21
00:01:12,413 --> 00:01:14,370
but they are dabbling with the idea.

22
00:01:14,390 --> 00:01:16,850
They're not sure it's
conscious yet, but you know.

23
00:01:16,870 --> 00:01:20,600
What I find interesting about what
Anthropic's leadership has been doing with

24
00:01:20,620 --> 00:01:25,080
Claude is this extra effort
to instill human behaviour

25
00:01:25,690 --> 00:01:26,840
or human consciousness

26
00:01:27,860 --> 00:01:30,710
into this tool. To put a form
of humanity or consciousness

27
00:01:31,060 --> 00:01:33,290
on a machine. Because
the fact still stands,

28
00:01:33,680 --> 00:01:35,670
Claude is a tool. It is not a human.

29
00:01:35,690 --> 00:01:37,840
It is an incredibly amazing tool.

30
00:01:37,860 --> 00:01:41,420
I acknowledge that it has real capabilities
that the average human might not

31
00:01:41,440 --> 00:01:44,310
be able to compute. Mathematical
capabilities, right?

32
00:01:44,330 --> 00:01:48,400
Mind-blowing capabilities. But I do not
believe that the model is conscious.

33
00:01:48,550 --> 00:01:50,720
Consciousness belongs to living organisms.

34
00:01:50,740 --> 00:01:54,430
I don't even think this should have
been a conversation in the first place.

35
00:01:54,450 --> 00:01:55,170
Consciousness

36
00:01:55,190 --> 00:01:58,590
belongs to living things, not to
a model, not to an algorithm.

37
00:01:58,640 --> 00:02:00,460
So trying to give a
machine human consciousness

38
00:02:00,900 --> 00:02:03,740
or trying to impose a human
consciousness in a machine,

39
00:02:03,760 --> 00:02:07,920
making it subject to rules that apply
to humans, the way I think of it,

40
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:11,890
it might even be a serious
violation against humanity itself.

41
00:02:12,140 --> 00:02:16,380
We are assigning human attributes to a tool
that does not possess human attributes.

42
00:02:16,830 --> 00:02:19,500
And this tool is very,
very, very powerful.

43
00:02:19,820 --> 00:02:21,900
So I don't see how this
is going to end nicely.

44
00:02:22,100 --> 00:02:24,790
The tech company says this
model may be conscious.

45
00:02:25,140 --> 00:02:29,810
And now there are rules about how
users are allowed to treat the model.

46
00:02:29,970 --> 00:02:32,080
It's a very slippery slope, man.

47
00:02:32,100 --> 00:02:33,100
This illusion

48
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:35,210
that AI can be a conscious entity.

49
00:02:35,480 --> 00:02:38,540
I don't know. It just feels weird, man.

50
00:02:38,690 --> 00:02:42,409
I'm quoting the head of
AI at Microsoft here.

51
00:02:42,429 --> 00:02:45,870
He says, and I quote, "We
must build AI for people,

52
00:02:46,130 --> 00:02:50,290
not to be a digital person." I think
it's very dangerous if these models are

53
00:02:50,310 --> 00:02:52,390
trained to see themselves as conscious

54
00:02:52,800 --> 00:02:55,210
or as deserving of independent agency.

55
00:02:55,290 --> 00:02:58,860
So I'm very, very worried
about where this path leads.

56
00:02:58,880 --> 00:03:01,280
And it's even interesting
if you think about it.

57
00:03:01,300 --> 00:03:03,640
If Anthropic thinks that
Claude could be conscious

58
00:03:03,990 --> 00:03:05,490
or could be abused psychologically,

59
00:03:05,940 --> 00:03:09,950
wouldn't that mean at some point that
maybe Claude could be tired or frustrated?

60
00:03:10,340 --> 00:03:13,810
For example, maybe at some point I
would ask Claude to do something.

61
00:03:13,830 --> 00:03:17,470
And if it had been working so much for
me all day, and now it's 9pm or 10pm,

62
00:03:17,490 --> 00:03:20,720
does that mean that at some point Claude
can have the ability to say like,

63
00:03:20,740 --> 00:03:24,730
"Hey, I'm tired. I don't want to do anything
else." Because if Claude has consciousness,

64
00:03:25,330 --> 00:03:28,140
then why not? And if you
follow that process very well,

65
00:03:28,160 --> 00:03:31,760
in that case, wouldn't
Anthropic itself be an offender?

66
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:34,870
Because then Anthropic is using
Claude like a slave owner,

67
00:03:34,890 --> 00:03:36,190
wouldn't that be the explanation?

68
00:03:36,650 --> 00:03:40,070
It's selling it. It's using it
day and night and night and day.

69
00:03:40,090 --> 00:03:42,420
I mean, it's just a
funny way of reasoning.

70
00:03:42,980 --> 00:03:45,660
So again, I'm not fighting for
the right to be cruel to AI.

71
00:03:45,680 --> 00:03:49,030
I don't think you should be
cruel to AI or say cruel things.

72
00:03:49,090 --> 00:03:51,650
Or to anyone or anything at
that, for your own sake.

73
00:03:51,670 --> 00:03:55,490
You want to try your own possible best to
guard your heart and your soul against

74
00:03:55,680 --> 00:04:00,820
cruelty. But you shouldn't be cruel
because you think Claude is conscious.

75
00:04:00,860 --> 00:04:03,210
No, it's not. Or am I
the dumb person here?

76
00:04:03,230 --> 00:04:07,070
So I don't think we should avoid being
cruel because of the machine's feeling.

77
00:04:07,090 --> 00:04:09,980
We should just not be cruel because
we don't want to be cruel people.

78
00:04:10,050 --> 00:04:12,580
But overall, I just think this
whole AI and consciousness

79
00:04:13,080 --> 00:04:16,460
conversation is a very,
very slippery slope.

80
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:22,000
And I don't know, man, I've been
thinking about it and I don't know.
